"Compares candidate actions by anticipated benefit and implementation effort to guide a first prioritization conversation.\n\nUse when: Screen a manageable backlog of improvement ideas. Distinguish promising low-effort actions from costly uncertain initiatives.\n\nAvoid when: Mandatory work or detailed economic appraisal cannot be reduced to the two axes.\n\nVisual grammar: Two axes use explicitly defined benefit and effort scales. Initiative markers or cards occupy the corresponding regions. Evidence and uncertainty tags prevent unsupported precision.\n\nBuild: Agree the outcome that impact refers to and the effort boundary. Estimate each idea using comparable assumptions and available evidence. Discuss dependencies and constraints before choosing actions from the grid.\n\nCheck: Impact refers to the same target outcome for every idea. Effort includes relevant implementation and adoption work. Regulatory or safety obligations are not deprioritized by convenience.\n\nPitfalls: Calling an idea a quick win without examining dependencies. Confusing implementation ease with confidence that the benefit will occur.\n\nExample: A library compares reminder messages, longer opening hours and self-service equipment by expected access benefit and total implementation effort.\n\nAlternatives: Weighted decision matrix, Assumption map."
